INT. BAT-GUANO MINE OFFICE - NIGHT

A timber shack bolted to a cavern wall. Dripping stalactites visible through a cracked window. A desk covered in ledgers, a kerosene lantern, a wall of hanging miner's lamps. The air is thick and sour.

INSPECTOR ALOYSIUS PRYCE, 50s, tall, thin, a tweed overcoat and a black umbrella, sits at the desk. His face is long and grave. He turns the pages of a ledger without hurry.

PHAEDRA LUND, 40s, the mine's owner, in a mud-spattered fur coat, watches him from the doorway. Her hair is silver, her mouth a razor.

MR. TANAKA, 60s, the foreman, round-faced and quiet, stands behind the desk with a pencil behind his ear. BRIGID, 20s, a clerk with red hair and ink-stained fingers, sorts receipts at a side table. OSWIN, 30s, a miner with soot to his eyes, sits on a barrel by the stove.

PRYCE
Mrs. Lund. Your figures are very tidy.

PHAEDRA
I pride myself on it.

PRYCE
Too tidy.

PHAEDRA
Is that an accusation?

PRYCE
It's an observation.

TANAKA
We keep good books, Inspector.

PRYCE
(not looking up)
Who keeps them?

BRIGID
I do, sir.

PRYCE
Alone?

BRIGID
Mostly.

PRYCE
Who checks your arithmetic?

BRIGID
Mr. Tanaka.

PRYCE
Mr. Tanaka, who checks yours?

TANAKA
Mrs. Lund.

PRYCE
And who checks hers?

PHAEDRA
God, Inspector.

Pryce nods. Closes the ledger. Opens another.

PRYCE
Oswin. You're the miner.

OSWIN
Yes, sir.

PRYCE
How many tonnes did you haul last week?

OSWIN
Forty.

PRYCE
The ledger says sixty.

OSWIN
Sixty is what they wrote.

PHAEDRA
Oswin.

OSWIN
I only count the barrows, ma'am.

Pryce looks at Phaedra. For a moment his face is the face of a priest, very patient. The kerosene lantern hisses.

PRYCE
Mrs. Lund, I'm going to ask Miss Brigid a question. You're welcome to stay.

PHAEDRA
I wouldn't miss it.

PRYCE
Miss Brigid. Where is the guano going?

BRIGID
To the dock. And then to a ship.

PRYCE
Which ship?

BRIGID
The Margaret Rose.

PRYCE
And where does the Margaret Rose go?

BRIGID
To Liverpool.

PRYCE
It does not.

Silence. A drop of water falls from a stalactite onto the roof.

PRYCE (CONT'D)
The Margaret Rose has been at the bottom of the Bay of Biscay since March.

Brigid's pen stops. Tanaka's pencil slips from behind his ear. Oswin stands up from the barrel, slowly. Phaedra, still in the doorway, smiles.

PHAEDRA
Then who, Inspector, has been buying my guano?
